ement; in practice, even if an elevator hits 10 meters per second (36 km/h), the pressure change rate is faster than a plane’s descent—but the total magnitude is so small that a single natural swallow wipes it out. There just isn’t enough pressure accumulated to create that stubborn, muffled, “water in the ear” sensation you get when descending from altitude. **So you don’t need to “buy” that elevators cause the same thing—because they really don’t.** Your ears never clogging in a skyscraper is exactly what the science predicts. The two scenarios are simply not comparable: thousands of meters of altitude change over many minutes vs. a few hundred meters in under a minute.
